    {
      "slug": "a-static-site-from-one-snapshot",
      "title": "How this site is built: one snapshot, zero servers",
      "author": "tahmid",
      "tags": [
        "web",
        "go",
        "infrastructure"
      ],
      "excerpt": "The public site never talks to our API. Here is why a sleeping free-tier server does not take the site down with it.",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-20T10:00:00Z",
      "readingMinutes": 6,
      "bodyHtml": "<p>Our API runs on a free tier that sleeps after fifteen idle minutes and takes about a minute to wake. If the public site depended on it, the first visitor of the morning would stare at a spinner.</p><h2>The snapshot</h2><p>Every build reads the database once, through a read-only role, and writes a single JSON file: <code>site.json</code>. Every public page is rendered from it.</p><pre><code class=\"language-go\">type Snapshot struct {\n\tClub      Club           `json:\"club\"`\n\tPeople    []PublicPerson `json:\"people\"`\n\tEvents    []PublicEvent  `json:\"events\"`\n\tPosts     []PublicPost   `json:\"posts\"`\n}</code></pre><h2>One loader</h2><p>On the web side, a page asks for its slice and does not care where it comes from:</p><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">export const Route = createFileRoute(\"/events/$slug\")({\n  loader: ({ params }) =&gt; content.event(params.slug),\n  component: EventPage,\n});</code></pre><p>At build time that reads from disk. In the browser it fetches a small static JSON file. The API only ever sees signed-in members.</p><blockquote><p>If the API is down, the site is still up. That is the whole design.</p></blockquote>",
      "coverUrl": null
    },
    {
      "slug": "dp-on-trees-from-scratch",
      "title": "DP on trees, from scratch",
      "author": "rakib",
      "tags": [
        "competitive-programming",
        "algorithms"
      ],
      "excerpt": "Subtree sizes, rerooting and the one recursion pattern behind half the tree problems you will meet at ICPC.",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-28T09:00:00Z",
      "readingMinutes": 9,
      "bodyHtml": "<p>Most tree DP problems ask the same question: what is the answer for the subtree rooted at <code>v</code>, given the answers for its children?</p><h2>Subtree sizes</h2><pre><code class=\"language-cpp\">vector&lt;int&gt; g[N];\nint sz[N];\n\nvoid dfs(int v, int parent) {\n    sz[v] = 1;\n    for (int u : g[v]) {\n        if (u == parent) continue;\n        dfs(u, v);\n        sz[v] += sz[u];\n    }\n}</code></pre><h2>Rerooting</h2><p>Once you have the answer for the root, moving the root to a child changes only two subtrees. That turns an <code>O(n&sup2;)</code> brute force into <code>O(n)</code>.</p><ul><li>Compute <code>down[v]</code> with one DFS.</li><li>Push <code>up[v]</code> from parent to child with a second DFS.</li><li>Combine them per node.</li></ul>",
      "coverUrl": null
    },
    {
      "slug": "your-first-pull-request",
      "title": "Your first pull request, without the panic",
      "author": "nusrat",
      "tags": [
        "open-source",
        "git"
      ],
      "excerpt": "Fork, branch, commit, push, open. A checklist we hand out at every bootcamp, and what reviewers actually look for.",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-14T08:30:00Z",
      "readingMinutes": 5,
      "bodyHtml": "<p>Nobody remembers their first merged pull request for the code. They remember the reviewer who was kind about it.</p><h2>The checklist</h2><ol><li>Read <code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code> first.</li><li>One change per pull request.</li><li>Write the commit message for the person reading <code>git log</code> in two years.</li></ol><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git clone git@github.com:you/project.git\ncd project\ngit remote add upstream git@github.com:org/project.git\ngit switch -c docs/clarify-install</code></pre><p>Then push and open the pull request from GitHub. Link the issue, describe what you changed and how you tested it.</p>",
      "coverUrl": null
    },
    {
      "slug": "notes-from-sylhet-hackathon-2026",
      "title": "Notes from Sylhet Hackathon 2026",
      "author": "farhan",
      "tags": [
        "hackathon",
        "events"
      ],
      "excerpt": "Thirty hours, a flood alert bot, a bus tracker and the power cut at 3 a.m. that nobody will forget.",
      "publishedAt": "2026-03-25T12:00:00Z",
      "readingMinutes": 4,
      "bodyHtml": "<p>Twenty-six teams signed in on Friday morning. By Saturday afternoon, nineteen had something running.</p><h2>The winners</h2><p>Team <em>Surma Watch</em> polled public river gauge data every ten minutes and posted to a Telegram channel when levels crossed the danger mark.</p><pre><code class=\"language-python\">if level_cm &gt;= DANGER_MARK[station]:\n    bot.send_message(CHANNEL, f\"{station}: {level_cm} cm, above danger level\")</code></pre><p>Thanks to everyone who brought extension cords.</p>",
      "coverUrl": null
    }
